## Proof

We show that for **every** $a \in \mathbb{R}$, $\int_0^\infty e^{2a W_s^2}\,ds = \infty$ almost surely. Consequently, there are **no** values of $a$ for which the inequality holds a.s.

---

**Case 1: $a \geq 0$.**  
Since $W_s^2 \geq 0$, we have $e^{2a W_s^2} \geq e^0 = 1$ for all $s \geq 0$. Hence
$$\int_0^\infty e^{2a W_s^2}\,ds \;\geq\; \int_0^\infty 1\,ds \;=\; \infty \quad\text{a.s.}$$

---

**Case 2: $a < 0$.**  
Fix any $\varepsilon > 0$. On the event $\{|W_s| \leq \varepsilon\}$ we have $W_s^2 \leq \varepsilon^2$, and since $a < 0$,
$$2a\,W_s^2 \;\geq\; 2a\,\varepsilon^2 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad e^{2a W_s^2} \;\geq\; e^{2a\varepsilon^2} \;>\; 0.$$
Therefore
$$\int_0^\infty e^{2a W_s^2}\,ds \;\geq\; e^{2a\varepsilon^2}\int_0^\infty \mathbf{1}_{\{|W_s|\leq \varepsilon\}}\,ds. \tag{$\ast$}$$

It remains to show that the occupation time $\int_0^\infty \mathbf{1}_{\{|W_s|\leq \varepsilon\}}\,ds = \infty$ a.s.

**Claim.** For one-dimensional Brownian motion and every $\varepsilon > 0$,
$$\int_0^\infty \mathbf{1}_{\{|W_s|\leq \varepsilon\}}\,ds \;=\; \infty \quad\text{a.s.}$$

*Proof of Claim.* By the occupation-times formula (Tanaka),
$$\int_0^t \mathbf{1}_{\{|W_s|\leq \varepsilon\}}\,ds \;=\; \int_{-\varepsilon}^{\varepsilon} L_t^x\,dx,$$
where $L_t^x$ is the local time of $W$ at level $x$. One-dimensional Brownian motion is **recurrent**: $\limsup_{t\to\infty} W_t = +\infty$ and $\liminf_{t\to\infty} W_t = -\infty$ a.s., so the path crosses every level $x$ infinitely often. A standard consequence of recurrence is that the local time at every level diverges:
$$L_\infty^x \;:=\; \lim_{t\to\infty} L_t^x \;=\; \infty \quad\text{a.s., for every } x \in \mathbb{R}.$$
(Indeed, each excursion from $x-\delta$ to $x+\delta$ contributes a positive amount to $L^x$, and there are infinitely many such excursions; by the strong Markov property these contributions are i.i.d. with positive mean, so their sum diverges a.s.)

Since $x \mapsto L_\infty^x$ is continuous (local time is continuous in the space variable) and $L_\infty^x = \infty$ for every $x \in [-\varepsilon,\varepsilon]$, we obtain
$$\int_{-\varepsilon}^{\varepsilon} L_\infty^x\,dx \;=\; \infty \quad\text{a.s.}$$
By monotone convergence, $\int_0^\infty \mathbf{1}_{\{|W_s|\leq\varepsilon\}}\,ds = \int_{-\varepsilon}^{\varepsilon} L_\infty^x\,dx = \infty$ a.s. ∎

Applying the Claim to $(\ast)$,
$$\int_0^\infty e^{2a W_s^2}\,ds \;\geq\; e^{2a\varepsilon^2}\cdot\infty \;=\; \infty \quad\text{a.s.}$$

---

**Conclusion.** Combining both cases, for every real number $a$,
$$\int_0^\infty e^{2a W_s^2}\,ds \;=\; \infty \quad\text{almost surely.}$$

The key mechanism is the **recurrence of one-dimensional Brownian motion**: the process spends an infinite Lebesgue amount of time in every neighborhood of the origin, where the integrand $e^{2a W_s^2}$ is bounded below by a strictly positive constant (for $a<0$), and is bounded below by $1$ (for $a\geq 0$). Hence the integral diverges for all $a$.

$$\boxed{\text{There are no values of } a \in \mathbb{R} \text{ for which } \int_0^\infty e^{2a W_s^2}\,ds < \infty \text{ a.s.}}$$

